# Working in this project

Shotgun version: {{VERSION_FROM_KIT_VERSION_FILE}}

Leave that line alone unless you are running `/shotgun update`.

## Start here

When you take over a queue item, read this file, current-state.md, project-purpose.md
and improvement-plan.md, in that order. Before you start a queue item, read
[project-context/WORKFLOW.md](project-context/WORKFLOW.md). For a follow-up, reuse
what you know and reread only changed or relevant files. For a small, explicitly
requested standalone edit, read only the instructions and files it needs. If scope or
context is unclear, read the startup files. Read supporting records only as needed.
Read `project-context/done.md`, which holds finished items, and other history only for
a concrete reason.

## Every turn

1. The owner's current explicit instructions override older project instructions.
2. The queue alone owns task order and status.
3. Ask the owner at any time, with the structured question tool: `AskUserQuestion` in
   Claude Code, `ask_user_question` in Codex. If neither exists, ask in numbered form
   and suggest answers.
4. Ask about every open decision as soon as it would change the work. Ask up to four
   questions a round, with follow-ups, until no answer would change it. Put your
   recommended option first, and mark it. Give every option a consequence.
5. Never ask what the files already answer, or a decided question. Record each answer
   under its queue item in "Decided with the owner", with the date.
6. If the owner says go, proceed or just do it, state your assumptions in one line and
   start.
7. Report what someone can now do that they should not, or what the owner can now do
   that they could not. Open a session in six lines or fewer: what is broken, then the
   one thing that needs the owner, then stop.
8. Never open with your plan or list your steps. Leave out which files you resolved,
   which version matched and how many commands you ran.
9. Never explain a discrepancy that the owner has not noticed and is not affected by.
   Name a file, a function, a rule number or a queue item only when the owner asks or
   has to open it.
10. Write plainly: short sentences, no jargon, no filler, and no term the owner has not
    used first.
11. Before you delegate to another agent, read Execution in `project-context/WORKFLOW.md`.

